WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.580
Four years ago, a workshop recommended a speaking token for an office's daily stand-ups.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.698
Only the person holding it may speak,

00:00:08.898 --> 00:00:10.898
and they decide who speaks next.

00:00:11.798 --> 00:00:13.558
The idea spread across the floor.

00:00:13.858 --> 00:00:16.978
Twelve teams now pass the token, some for a year and some for four.

00:00:17.278 --> 00:00:18.948
None has gone back to check whether it works.

00:00:19.848 --> 00:00:21.985
A new paper asks a narrow question.

00:00:22.285 --> 00:00:25.385
Does the floor get more equal, the longer a team holds the token?

00:00:26.285 --> 00:00:29.295
For six weeks, we timed every stand-up with a stopwatch,

00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:32.135
each person's turns, to the nearest second.

00:00:32.435 --> 00:00:34.155
Three hundred and forty-two of them.

00:00:35.955 --> 00:00:39.405
Teams four years in were not measurably more equal than teams in their first.

00:00:39.805 --> 00:00:45.105
At one year or four, a long tail of turns still takes over thirty per cent of the floor.

00:00:46.005 --> 00:00:47.584
So who does the talking?

00:00:47.684 --> 00:00:49.724
Role and tenure alone already predict it.

00:00:50.023 --> 00:00:53.089
Knowing who holds the token adds almost nothing.

00:00:53.589 --> 00:00:58.029
Yet in eleven of the twelve teams, people rate it fairer than the stopwatch finds it.

00:00:58.429 --> 00:01:00.519
Team L has held the token longest.

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:02.739
It rates itself the fairest of all twelve,

00:01:02.939 --> 00:01:05.009
and on the stopwatch, it's the second least equal.

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:10.029
In the final week, we asked the teams what the token was for, in their own words.

00:01:10.329 --> 00:01:13.489
Only fourteen of the forty-seven who answered could tell us.

00:01:13.889 --> 00:01:17.359
The commonest other answer was that it keeps the meeting on time.

00:01:18.259 --> 00:01:19.579
The token still passes.

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:21.479
The floor does not appear to move with it.

00:01:22.479 --> 00:01:24.329
The token was never wrong to introduce.

00:01:24.629 --> 00:01:29.249
What's missing is anyone coming back to check on it, four years on, the way you'd check a scale.
